WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:05.760
Cross two pure-breeding fruit fly lines.

2
00:00:06.660 --> 00:00:07.660
Both have white eyes.

3
00:00:08.440 --> 00:00:10.620
Yet the offspring can have ordinary red eyes.

4
00:00:11.520 --> 00:00:15.380
How can that be? A red fly eye needs the work of many genes.

5
00:00:16.440 --> 00:00:18.540
Enzymes make the pigments in several steps.

6
00:00:19.560 --> 00:00:21.840
Transporters carry them into pigment granules.

7
00:00:22.800 --> 00:00:25.100
Losing some of these genes leaves the eye white.

8
00:00:26.083 --> 00:00:28.403
Think of an assembly line with five stations.

9
00:00:29.303 --> 00:00:31.683
A broken station anywhere stops the product.

10
00:00:32.523 --> 00:00:35.183
So every breakdown looks the same at the end of the line.

11
00:00:36.083 --> 00:00:37.783
One line has station two broken.

12
00:00:38.723 --> 00:00:40.343
Another has station four broken.

13
00:00:41.283 --> 00:00:44.343
Borrow the working stations from each, and you get a working line.

14
00:00:45.183 --> 00:00:48.223
If both lines broke station two, borrowing helps nothing.

15
00:00:49.283 --> 00:00:50.703
Now do the same with genes.

16
00:00:51.943 --> 00:00:55.963
Combine the two mutations in one diploid, one on each homologous chromosome.

17
00:00:56.823 --> 00:00:58.383
This arrangement is called trans.

18
00:00:59.403 --> 00:01:05.583
One mutation is in gene A. The other is in gene B. Each chromosome supplies the

19
00:01:05.583 --> 00:01:07.203
working allele that the other lacks.

20
00:01:08.083 --> 00:01:09.523
The phenotype is wild type.

21
00:01:09.523 --> 00:01:11.563
The mutations complement.

22
00:01:12.617 --> 00:01:15.857
Now try two alleles of one gene, gene A, in trans.

23
00:01:16.857 --> 00:01:22.037
Each chromosome carries a broken copy of gene A. The diploid has no working copy.

24
00:01:22.917 --> 00:01:23.917
So it stays mutant.

25
00:01:24.737 --> 00:01:26.337
The mutations fail to complement.

26
00:01:27.337 --> 00:01:31.777
Mutations that fail to complement one another form a complementation group.

27
00:01:32.617 --> 00:01:34.977
In the simple model, that group is one gene.

28
00:01:35.950 --> 00:01:37.390
Here is a worked problem.

29
00:01:38.350 --> 00:01:41.890
Six recessive white-eyed mutants are crossed in all fifteen pairs.

30
00:01:42.910 --> 00:01:47.070
Four pairs stay white: one in three, one in five, two in six, three in five.

31
00:01:48.110 --> 00:01:49.650
Mutant 1 fails with 3 and 5.

32
00:01:50.530 --> 00:01:52.410
So 1, 3 and 5 form a group.

33
00:01:53.330 --> 00:01:54.970
Mutant 2 fails only with 6.

34
00:01:55.850 --> 00:01:57.030
Those two form a group.

35
00:01:57.870 --> 00:01:59.710
Mutant 4 complements all the others.

36
00:02:00.450 --> 00:02:03.610
That makes three complementation groups, so at least three genes.

37
00:02:03.610 --> 00:02:07.410
It is at least, because other genes may not have been hit.

38
00:02:08.383 --> 00:02:09.863
The test has a condition.

39
00:02:10.843 --> 00:02:12.663
Both mutations must be recessive.

40
00:02:13.343 --> 00:02:18.343
A dominant allele gives a mutant phenotype opposite any partner, so the trans result

41
00:02:18.343 --> 00:02:21.883
says nothing. For recessive mutations, keep this rule.

42
00:02:22.763 --> 00:02:25.943
In the simple model, wild type in trans means different genes.

43
00:02:27.183 --> 00:02:28.603
Mutant in trans means one gene.
